[FINAL-TEXT] Confirmed. The DROP rules on eth2 with 13 ICMP + 26 UDP packets hit prove the traffic is being dropped.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Router6 has a misconfigured iptables firewall on its FORWARD chain that drops all forwarded traffic originating from its WAN-side interface (eth2, connecting to router3). The chain contains four rules: a narrow ACCEPT for TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, followed by catch-all DROP rules for TCP, UDP, and all other protocols (including ICMP) — all scoped to inbound traffic on eth2. Since every packet from the desktop network (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24) arrives at router6 via eth2 after transiting router0→router1→router2→router3, these DROP rules silently discard ICMP, UDP, and TCP before the packets can be forwarded to the laptop network (172.18.50.0/24 on switch11 via mswitch2). This is why router6 can ping laptop4 directly (that traffic uses the OUTPUT chain, bypassing FORWARD entirely) while pc4 and all other devices upstream of router3 cannot reach laptop4 or laptop5 at all. The hit counters confirm it: rule 3 (DROP UDP) shows 26 packets matching — the traceroute UDP probes — and rule 4 (DROP all/ICMP) shows 13 packets matching — the ICMP echo requests from the ping tests. Root cause: router6's FORWARD chain has blanket DROP rules for all protocols entering on eth2, blocking the entire desktop-to-laptop traffic path. Corrective fix: remove the three DROP rules (`iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-p udp`, and `-p 0`) and replace them with policy-aligned ACCEPT rules, or at minimum insert `iptables -I FORWARD 1 -p icmp -j ACCEPT` and appropriate stateful rules (`-m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED`) to restore bidirectional connectivity.
=== END ===